 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "A solar water heater (solar collector) uses the sun's energy to heat water. A dark (black) metal plate under glass absorbs sunlight; water flows in pipes over the plate and gets hot; the hot water rises into an insulated tank.\nWhy black? Dark surfaces absorb heat radiation best; shiny or white surfaces reflect it. Why glass? It lets sunlight in and keeps heat inside (like a greenhouse). Why insulation? To stop heat escaping.\nEnergy: energy (J) = power (W) × time (s). A panel receiving 500 W for 60 s gets 30 000 J.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "P": 500,
   "t": 120
  },
  "problem": "A solar collector receives {P} W of sunlight. How much energy does it receive in {t} s?",
  "steps": [
   "Energy = power × time.",
   "{P} × {t} = {energyJ(P, t)} J."
  ],
  "answer": "{energyJ(P, t)} J",
  "checks": []
 },
